Yes. The deeper I look, the more I think **robot parts + physical robotics economics should be one of POW’s flagship gardens**.

The strongest version is not “track robotics companies.” It is:

> **Track the physical cost and availability of building, importing, operating and repairing robots — beginning in China and ending in the UK.**

That gives POW a natural connection to equities, UK jobs, trade, repair, materials, compute and actual bottlenecks.

### The geographic scope is clearer now

Do **China + UK deeply**, not “the whole world” superficially.

China is already 54% of global industrial-robot installations in the latest comparable IFR data: 295,000 installations during 2024. Chinese manufacturers also crossed 50% domestic market share. ([IFR International Federation of Robotics][1])

And Chinese production is accelerating dramatically. China's National Bureau of Statistics reports **773,074 industrial robots produced in 2025, +28% YoY**. January–July 2026 production reached **635,056, +28.5% YoY**. ([National Bureau of Statistics][2])

Meanwhile the UK installed only **2,500 industrial robots in 2024**, down from the unusual 3,800-unit 2023 tax-incentive spike. ([IFR International Federation of Robotics][1])

That's fantastic for our purposes:

```text
CHINA
production
component pricing
OEM pricing
suppliers
robot BOMs
domestic substitution
exports
capacity
listed component companies
        ↓
transport / FX / tariffs / distributors
        ↓
UK landed price
UK inventory
UK integrators
UK deployments
UK jobs
UK repairs
UK used market
        ↓
UK listed-company effects
```

You effectively get a **robotics transmission mechanism**.

