# The open-source robot data is much better than I realised

There is now a GitHub organisation called `robot-descriptions` whose purpose is explicitly to build a massive open collection of robot descriptions. Its Python package gives access to **190+ robot descriptions** across frameworks. ([GitHub][8])

There is also a research dataset containing URDF bundles and manufacturer/type metadata. ([GitHub][9])

Then we have manufacturer/ecosystem repos.

FANUC itself publishes its ROS2 descriptions and meshes by robot family. ([GitHub][10])

ROS-Industrial has large ABB and FANUC repositories. ([GitHub][11])

The KUKA descriptions are particularly useful: links, joints, inertial properties, velocity/effort limits, robot family, payload/reach naming and control interfaces are explicitly encoded. ([GitHub][12])

We can therefore bootstrap a **Robot Knowledge Graph** almost for free:

```text
manufacturer
 → family
 → model
 → payload
 → reach
 → axes
 → links
 → joints
 → controller
 → application
 → accessories
 → compatible equipment
```

Then gradually attach the real physical parts.

