# Parts are where POW could become genuinely differentiated

There are already enormous generic parts businesses, but nobody I found has the combination we're contemplating.

Nexar/Octopart has **70+ million electronic parts**, with distributor stock, prices, lifecycle and lead-time information updated daily. ([Nexar Support][13])

Its higher-end supply-chain data even contains **weekly and monthly historical global inventory**, estimated days of supply and availability-window metrics. ([Nexar Support][14])

Findchips also provides real-time inventory/pricing and already exposes price/inventory trends. ([Findchips][15])

So we should **use those**, not try to recreate Octopart.

But electronics are only part of robots.

The really attractive garden is:

```text
precision gearbox
servo motor
servo drive
encoder
bearing
linear motion
force/torque sensor
camera
LiDAR
safety scanner
controller
PLC
industrial PC
CPU/GPU
DRAM
SSD
power electronics
connectors
cabling
gripper / EOAT
battery
```

Generic electronics databases become one input into this robot-specific graph.

