## China gives us a direct price layer too

LCSC has an official API that returns component details and supports prices denominated in **CNY**, as well as USD/EUR/HKD. ([LCSC Electronics][16])

Farnell/element14 has a proper REST API giving UK regional pricing, stock, status and lead time; their catalogue is over **1.3 million products**. ([element14 Partner Network][17])

That immediately creates:

```text
same MPN

LCSC China CNY
       ↓
FX
       ↓
theoretical landed cost
       ↓
Farnell UK GBP
       ↓
UK markup / scarcity premium
```

That **China→UK spread history** sounds like a very POW-native dataset.

For mechanical components where exact MPN matching is harder, I'd additionally snapshot Made-in-China, Alibaba/1688 where permitted, OEM catalogues and specialist distributors. Made-in-China already exposes quoted price ranges, MOQ, suppliers and manufacturing categories publicly. ([Made-in-China][18])

Alibaba has an Open Platform, although its APIs are more seller/partner-oriented than a general-purpose market-data feed, so I wouldn't make our architecture depend on it. ([OpenAPI][19])

